e:\n            if v is None:\n                return None\n            value = int(v)\n            if value < minimum:\n                raise ValueError(f\"{v!r} must be >= {minimum}\")\n            return value\n\n        return EnvVar(_init)\n```\n\nwith a name-aware version:\n\n```\n    @staticmethod\n    def int_or_none_ge(*, minimum: int, name: str):\n        \"\"\"可选整数配置：未设置时为 None；设置时用 int 解析且不得小于 minimum。\"\"\"\n\n        def _init(v: str | None):\n            if v is None:\n                return None\n            try:\n                value = int(v)\n            except (TypeError, ValueError) as exc:\n                raise ValueError(f\"{name} must be an integer >= {minimum}, got {v!r}\") from exc\n            if value < minimum:\n                raise ValueError(f\"{name} must be an integer >= {minimum}, got {v!r}\")\n            return value\n\n        return EnvVar(_init)\n```\n\nNote: empty string `int(\"\")` raises ValueError → good, with name.\n\nAnd the config line: `DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS = EnvVar.int_or_none_ge(minimum=32, name=\"DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS\")`.\n\nHmm wait: does the descriptor's __get__ set the value via `self._init(...)`? My factory returns `EnvVar(_init)` — need to know EnvVar's constructor signature! The existing factories do `return EnvVar(_init)` (from the matched old_text: `        return EnvVar(_init)\\n\\n\\nclass envs:`). So EnvVar takes one arg (in vLLM it's `EnvField` with `_init` alias). Good — my edit reused that exact pattern.\n\nBut careful: after edit the config's `__set_name__`? Whatever, reuse pattern.\n\nAlso unset detection: in vLLM `__get__` does `val = os.environ.get(self.name)`; if None and self.default is not sentinel → default. My EnvVar has no default → returns _init(None). Fine.\n\nOne risk: does `__get__` wrap exceptions? If it does `raise RuntimeError(...)`, my ValueError becomes RuntimeError — test may accept? Unknown. But my ValueError message includes name either way.\n\nAlso caching: